{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-749c8f58a5d14ebdb735b84f92c450d7","ordinal":441,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.200","text":"\n\nLikewise with an argument of this kind: “If it is day, it is light; but it is not light; therefore it is not day.” From the fact that day is not observed without light, the conditional “If it is day, it is light” might be thought sound. If, hypothetically, it should ever appear to be day and yet not light, the conditional would be called false. But as far as the indemonstrable just mentioned goes, the fact that it is not day when there is no light is concluded by means of “If it is day, it is light.” So each of them, to be confirmed, needs the other to have been firmly grasped first, so that it may become credible through it, in the reciprocal mode."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7ce7c3348ec5486280c8aa04e43ce1ca","ordinal":442,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.201","text":"\n\nAgain, some things cannot hold together with one another, day and night for instance. From this fact the negated conjunction, “Not both: it is day and it is night,” and the disjunction, “Either it is day or it is night,” might be thought sound. But they think that the ⟨not⟩ holding together of these things is confirmed by the negated conjunction and by the disjunction. They say: “Not both: it is day and it is night; but it is night; therefore it is not day”; and “Either it is day or it is night; but it is night; therefore it is not day”; or “but it is not night; therefore it is day.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-270f115ebbe249a8993db6921d9c92fc","ordinal":443,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.202","text":"\n\nFrom this we in turn infer the following. To confirm the disjunction and the negated conjunction, we need to have apprehended beforehand that the propositions contained in them cannot hold together. Yet they think they conclude that these cannot hold together by means of the disjunction and the negated conjunction. So the reciprocal mode is brought in. We cannot trust the leading premises just mentioned without apprehending that the propositions contained in them cannot hold together. Nor can we affirm that they cannot hold together before the syllogisms have been propounded by means of these leading premises."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4048f8a9a9e94e3e816339ec94b1740f","ordinal":444,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.203","text":"\n\nHence, since this circling back leaves us nowhere to begin our trust, we will say that neither the third, nor the fourth, nor the fifth indemonstrable has any reality, as far as these considerations go. This much will suffice on syllogisms too, for the present."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3734bd6b4efe4836bd37ba6c54a718a5","ordinal":445,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.204","text":"\n\nI think the method of induction too can easily be dismissed. By means of it they want to establish the universal from the particulars. So they will do this by surveying either all the particulars or some of them. If some, the induction will be insecure, since some of the particulars left out of the induction may conflict with the universal. If all, they will be toiling at the impossible, since the particulars are infinite and unbounded. So in this way, I think, induction turns out to be shaken from both sides."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e64825b72a254833adc169ed2b051810","ordinal":446,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.205","text":"\n\nThe dogmatists also pride themselves on their technical treatment of definitions, which they count as part of the logical branch of what they call philosophy. So let us say a few things about definitions too, for now. The dogmatists think definitions are useful for many things. But there are two topmost headings ⟨which⟩"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f67b9fa0d9e84c14a97af3170eeee724","ordinal":447,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.206","text":"\n\nthey say comprise the whole of their necessity, as you will perhaps find. In every case they show definitions to be necessary either for apprehension or for teaching. So if we point out that they are useful for neither of these, we will, I think, overturn all the futile labor the dogmatists have spent on them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-22a48aa79c8140be885a659a6a88f6c0","ordinal":448,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.207","text":"\n\nTo begin with, then: someone who does not know the thing to be defined cannot define what he does not know. And someone who knows it and then defines it has not apprehended the thing defined from the definition. He has added the definition to something already apprehended. If so, definition is not necessary for the apprehension of things. Indeed, if we want to define everything, we will define nothing at all, because of the infinite regress. If we admit that some things are apprehended even without definitions, we show that definitions are not necessary for apprehension, since we could apprehend everything without definitions in the same way the undefined things were apprehended. So either we will define nothing at all"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7960ebc5ca65490490079972c6fd07ed","ordinal":449,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.208","text":"\n\nbecause of the [infinite regress], or we will show that definitions are not necessary. For these reasons we would not find them necessary for teaching either. The first person who came to know the thing knew it without a definition. In the same way, the one being taught it can be taught it without a definition."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-55df1bf59cf740ffb72e6183957226fc","ordinal":450,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.209","text":"\n\nMoreover, they judge definitions by the things defined. They say that definitions are faulty if they contain something that does not belong to the things defined, either to all of them or to some. So when someone says that a human is a rational immortal animal, or a rational mortal literate animal, they say the definition is faulty. In the first case no human is immortal, and in the second some are not literate."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d9c3194a97414a2a993fac500d69498f","ordinal":451,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.210","text":"\n\nBut perhaps definitions cannot be judged at all, because the particulars by which they ought to be judged are infinite. And then they could not give us apprehension and teaching of the very things by which they are judged. Those things, clearly, have been recognized and apprehended beforehand, if at all. How would it not be ridiculous to say that definitions are useful for apprehension, or teaching, or clarity of any kind, when they wheel in so much unclarity on us?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b467c9aac1dd449291acbfa82e335eb1","ordinal":452,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.211","text":"\n\nFor example—to have a little fun—suppose someone wanted to find out from somebody whether he had met a man riding a horse and leading a dog, and put the question like this: “Rational mortal animal, receptive of intellect and knowledge, have you encountered a laughter-capable, flat-nailed animal, receptive of political knowledge, with its hemispheres seated upon a mortal neighing animal, leading a four-footed barking animal?” How would he not be a laughing-stock, leaving the man speechless about so familiar a thing because of his definitions? So we must say that, as far as these considerations go, definition is useless,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2318b7c4a3a44a85b1fce123dc753dba","ordinal":453,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.212","text":"\n\nwhether it is said to be an account that leads us, by means of a brief reminder, to a conception of the things the words stand for—as is clear, isn’t it, from what we said a little earlier—or an account that reveals what it is to be something, or whatever anyone likes. In trying to establish what a definition is, they fall into an endless disagreement. I pass over it now, in keeping with the plan of this work, since ⟨what we have been saying here about definition being useless⟩ seems to overturn definitions anyway. That is enough for me to have said about definitions for now."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6a4aa5eb59004606ae0a37b7c3f30973","ordinal":454,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.213","text":"\n\nSome of the dogmatists say that dialectic is a science of syllogizing, inducing, defining ⟨and dividing⟩. We have already discussed, after the arguments about the criterion, the sign and proof, syllogisms, induction and definitions. So we think it is not out of place to deal briefly with division too. They say, then, that division happens in four ways. A name is divided into its meanings, or a whole into parts, or a genus into species, or a species into individuals. That there is no science of division in any of these cases is perhaps easy to go through."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0b29d754f36245abbd1a43846d602699","ordinal":455,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.214","text":"\n\nFirst, then, they say that sciences concern what is by nature and not at all what is by convention, and reasonably so. Science aims to be something firm and unchangeable, whereas what is by convention changes readily and easily, altering with changes of convention, which are up to us. Now, names signify by convention and not by nature. Otherwise everyone, Greeks and non-Greeks alike, would understand everything signified by words. Besides, it is up to us to indicate and signify the things signified by whatever names we like, ⟨and⟩ at any time. So how could there be a science that divides a name into its meanings? Or how could dialectic be, as some think, a science of things that signify and things signified?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d49e8fbd4e7e4a2caa000fc90314f60a","ordinal":456,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.215","text":"\n\nWe will discuss whole and part in what are called the physical sections too. For now, the following must be said about the so-called division of a whole into its parts. When someone says that ten is divided into one, two, three and four, ten is not divided into these. For as soon as its first part is removed—to grant this for now by concession—the one, say, ten is no longer there, but nine, and in general something other than ten."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1770e330635c4a8eaf2394ede5cfc4d9","ordinal":457,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.216","text":"\n\nSo the removal and division of the rest is made not from ten but from other numbers, which change with each removal. Perhaps, then, it is not possible to divide the whole into what are said to be its parts. For if the whole is divided into parts, the parts must be contained in the whole before the division. But perhaps they are not contained in it. For instance—to rest the argument on ten again—they say that nine is certainly a part of ten, since ten is divided into one and nine. But so is eight, likewise, since ten is divided into eight and two. And likewise seven, six, five, four, three, two and one."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d33d91fbec7247fe99cffb8a4a1327ff","ordinal":458,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.217","text":"\n\nSo if all these are contained in ten, and added together with it they make fifty-five, then fifty-five is contained in ten, which is absurd. So the things said to be its parts are not contained in ten. Nor can ten be divided into them as a whole into parts, since they are not observed in it at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b0ea5140b11845b6b1cb552bdc377ed2","ordinal":459,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.218","text":"\n\nThe same will happen with magnitudes too, when someone wants to divide, say, a magnitude of ten cubits. So perhaps it is not possible to divide a whole into parts either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1408c75a191a439eade2bc1a889bec1f","ordinal":460,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.219","text":"\n\nThere remains the argument about genera and species. We will discuss it more fully elsewhere, but for now we will put it briefly. If they say that genera and species are concepts, the arguments against the ruling part and against impressions overturn them. But if they grant them a reality of their own, what will they say to this?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8400ad8a6cc24a53b48d6b4e4cb1590e","ordinal":461,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.220","text":"\n\nIf there are genera, either they are as many as the species, or there is one [species, that is,] genus common to all the species said to belong to it. If the genera are as many as their species, there will no longer be a common genus to be divided into them. If one genus is said to be in all its species, then each of its species participates either in the whole of it or in a part of it. But not in the whole. It is impossible for something that is one to be contained in this thing and that in the same way, so as to be observed as a whole in each of the things it is said to be in. And if in a part, then, first, the whole genus will not follow on the species, as they suppose. A human will not be an animal but part of an animal—substance, for instance, but neither animate nor capable of perception."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e84cf45ec21f416ca92b445e4b60d8eb","ordinal":462,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.221","text":"\n\nNext, it would be said that all the species participate either in the same part of their genus or in different parts. But not in the same part, for the reasons just given. If in different parts, the species will not be similar to one another in genus, which they will not accept. And each genus will be infinite, being cut into infinitely many pieces—not only into the species but into the individuals too, in which it is observed along with its species. For Dion is said to be not only a human but an animal too. If these consequences are absurd, the species do not participate in a part of their genus either, if it is one."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a1f0f7132b5b474c91931058334c24a0","ordinal":463,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.222","text":"\n\nIf each species participates neither in the whole of the genus nor in a part of it, how could one genus be said to be in all its species, so as to be divided into them? Perhaps no one could say, short of inventing some phantoms. And those will be overturned by the dogmatists’ own undecidable disagreements, following the skeptical methods."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-203d4f2c336b48f8a578f52f6be7f8a1","ordinal":464,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.223","text":"\n\nIn addition, the following must be said. Species are of this sort or of that sort. Their genera are either of both this sort and that sort, or of this sort but not that, or of neither this sort nor that. For instance, among somethings, some are bodies and others incorporeal, some true and others false, some white, say, and others black, some very large and others very small, and so on. Then the “something,” to take an example, which some say is the most generic of all, will be either all of these, or one side only, or none.\n〔1〕 The Stoics made “something” (ti) their highest genus, covering both bodies and incorporeals."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-db80f4adde2b41748e02a65e49efa445","ordinal":465,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.224","text":"\n\nIf the something is nothing at all, the genus is nothing either, and the inquiry is at an end. If it should be said to be all of them, then, besides the claim being impossible, each of the species and individuals it is in will have to be all of them too. Animal, as they say, is animate, perceiving substance, and so each of its species is said to be substance, animate and perceiving. In the same way, if the genus is both body and incorporeal, false and true, black, say, and white, smallest and largest, and everything else, then each of the species and individuals will be all of these, which is not observed. So this is false too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-336687e2433e4410ad18dab7d72d3888","ordinal":466,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.225","text":"\n\nIf it is one side only, the genus of these will not be the genus of the rest. If the something is body, for instance, it will not be the genus of incorporeals; and if animal is rational, it will not be the genus of non-rational animals. Then there will be no incorporeal something and no non-rational animal, and likewise in other cases, which is absurd. So the genus can be neither of both this sort and that, nor of this sort but not that, nor indeed of neither this sort nor that [as a genus]. If so, there is no genus at all. If someone says that the genus is all things potentially, we will say that what is potentially something must also be something actually. For instance, no one can be potentially literate unless he is also something actually. So if the genus is all things potentially, we ask them what it is actually, and so the same difficulties remain. For it cannot be all the contraries actually."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c13729789fd54c4099edadc05fe6a262","ordinal":467,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.226","text":"\n\nNor can it be some things actually and others only potentially—body actually, say, and incorporeal potentially. For what is potentially something is what can come to exist actually. But what is actually body cannot be incorporeal in actuality. So if, for the sake of argument, something is body, it is not incorporeal potentially, and vice versa. So the genus cannot be some things actually and others only potentially. And if it is nothing at all actually, it does not even exist. So the genus which they say they divide into species is nothing."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9ee5d4a53e0b4748a0dd81f9e13ba6a3","ordinal":468,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.227","text":"\n\nThis too is worth considering. Alexander and Paris are the same person, so it cannot be that “Alexander is walking” is true while “Paris is walking” is false. In the same way, if being human is the same thing for Theon and for Dion, the name “human,” brought into the structure of a proposition, will make the proposition either true for both or false for both. But this is not what we observe. When Dion is sitting and Theon walking, “A human is walking” is true said of one and false said of the other. So the name “human” is not common to both, nor the same for both, but, if anything, peculiar to each.\n〔1〕 Alexander is another name for Paris, the Trojan prince of the Iliad."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ef70566537f045eea9cf11f3c2cc6100","ordinal":469,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.228","text":"\n\nSimilar things are said about common accidents too. Suppose one and the same accident, seeing, belongs to both Dion and Theon. If, hypothetically, Dion dies while Theon survives and sees, they will say either that the sight of the dead Dion remains undestroyed, which is incongruous, ⟨or⟩ that the same sight has been destroyed and not destroyed, which is absurd. So Theon’s sight is not the same as Dion’s, but, if anything, each has his own. Again, if the same accident, breathing, belongs to both Dion and Theon, it cannot be that the breathing in Theon exists while the breathing in Dion does not. Yet it can, when one has died and the other survives. So it is not the same. That will do for now as a brief treatment of these matters."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c4a71d45322044e3a2506c81289668fe","ordinal":470,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.229","text":"\n\nPerhaps it is not out of place to dwell briefly on the subject of sophisms as well, since those who glorify dialectic say it is necessary for resolving these too. For if, they say, dialectic can tell true arguments from false ones, and sophisms are false arguments, it will also be able to discern these, which corrupt the truth with apparent plausibility. So the dialecticians, as though coming to the aid of a tottering life, earnestly try to teach us the concept of sophisms, their varieties, and their solutions. They say that a sophism is a persuasive argument, cunningly crafted so as to get one to accept a conclusion that is either false, or similar to a falsehood, or non-evident, or otherwise unacceptable."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fa8d0b8a8fc94aacb4464ea305b5d033","ordinal":471,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.230","text":"\n\nFalse, as in this case: “No one gives a predicate to drink; to drink wormwood is a predicate; therefore no one gives wormwood to drink.” Similar to a falsehood, as in this case: “What neither was possible nor is possible is not absurd; it neither was possible nor is possible that the doctor, insofar as he is a doctor, commits murder; ⟨therefore it is not absurd that the doctor, insofar as he is a doctor, commits murder⟩.”\n〔1〕 In Stoic logic a predicate such as “to drink wormwood” is an incorporeal “sayable,” not something one can drink; wormwood was a bitter medicinal drink."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-24f16ece6e28415aa2fec62af1e4f2d2","ordinal":472,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.231","text":"\n\nNon-evident, thus: “It is not the case both that I have asked you something first and that the stars are not even in number; but I have asked you something first; therefore the stars are even in number.” Otherwise unacceptable, like the so-called solecistic arguments, for instance: “What you look at exists; you look at him raving; therefore him raving exists.” Or: “What you see exists; you see an inflamed place; therefore exists an inflamed place.”\n〔1〕 In Greek these conclusions keep the object case from the premise and so are ungrammatical; the English imitates the error."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-64ea2332aeaa479085107fee8511da71","ordinal":473,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.232","text":"\n\nNext they try [to see, that is,] to set out their solutions. Of the first sophism they say that one thing has been granted in the premises and another inferred. What has been granted is that a predicate is not drunk, and that to drink wormwood, not wormwood itself, is a predicate. So the conclusion ought to have been “Therefore no one drinks to-drink-wormwood,” which is true. Instead, what was inferred was “Therefore no one drinks wormwood,” which is false and does not follow from the premises granted."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3eda4e5951c040dc85786af9f4e17ae3","ordinal":474,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.233","text":"\n\nOf the second, they say that it seems to lead to a falsehood, so that it makes the unwary hesitate to assent to it, but that it concludes something true: “Therefore it is not absurd that the doctor, insofar as he is a doctor, commits murder.” For no proposition is absurd, and “The doctor, insofar as he is a doctor, commits murder” is a proposition. So this is not absurd either. The argument that leads to something non-evident, they say,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-18e0e7a08bcc489b84bd22f1ce3d1d05","ordinal":475,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.234","text":"\n\nbelongs to the class of changing arguments. When nothing has been asked beforehand, as the hypothesis requires, the negated conjunction is true. For the conjunction is false, since the falsehood “I have asked you something first” is part of it. But once the negated conjunction has been asked, the additional premise “But I have asked you something first” becomes true. Because the negated conjunction was asked before the additional premise, the negated conjunction becomes false, since the false member of the conjunction has become true. So the conclusion can never be drawn, since the negated conjunction never holds together with the additional premise."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1ec8c92887f1468c96965432c47cb356","ordinal":476,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.235","text":"\n\nAs for the last kind, the solecistic arguments, some say that their conclusions are drawn absurdly, contrary to ordinary usage. That is the kind of thing some dialecticians say about sophisms (others say other things). This may perhaps tickle the ears of the more careless, but it is superfluous, and the effort spent on it is wasted. This can perhaps be seen even from what we have already said. We pointed out that the true and the false cannot be apprehended on the dialecticians’ principles. We did so in various other ways, and also by overturning the witnesses to their syllogistic ability, namely proof and the indemonstrable arguments."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c39405440f2e4827822605c47b6093eb","ordinal":477,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.236","text":"\n\nOn the topic before us in particular, there is much else that could be said, but for now let us say this briefly. The sophisms that dialectic seems peculiarly able to refute are ones whose solution is useless. Those whose solution is useful the dialectician would not solve. They are solved by the people in each art who have a close acquaintance with the matters in question."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c39e1e01e1284739b20915c1c2d55685","ordinal":478,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.237","text":"\n\nTo mention one or two examples, suppose a sophism like this were put [to a doctor]: “In illnesses, a varied diet and wine should be approved during the abatements; in every type of illness an abatement certainly occurs before the first three-day period; therefore it is necessary, for the most part, to take a varied diet and wine before the first three-day period.” The dialectician would have nothing to say toward solving the argument, useful though the solution is,\n〔1〕 The “three-day period” (diatritos) was a key interval in Methodist medicine, by which treatment was timed."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-34136c66828a4acbb4590a21f2c27e7d","ordinal":479,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.238","text":"\n\nbut the doctor will solve the sophism. He knows that “abatement” is used in two senses: the abatement of the illness as a whole, and the turn for the better after the peak of each partial intensification. He knows that before the first three-day period it is mostly the abatement of a partial intensification that occurs, whereas we approve a varied diet not in that, but in the abatement of the illness as a whole. So he will say that the premises of the argument are disconnected. One abatement is taken in the first premise, namely that of the whole condition, and another in the second, namely the partial one."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1212b5a1b8484f879c81c8a3c713a556","ordinal":480,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.239","text":"\n\nAgain, suppose that, in the case of someone feverish because of an intense condensation, an argument like this were put: “Opposites are cures of opposites; cold is opposite to the present heat; therefore cold is suited to the present heat.” The dialectician will keep quiet. But the doctor,\n〔1〕 In Methodist theory every disease was a state of constriction or of relaxation, to be treated by its opposite."}]}